# Large assets moved to Obsidian vault (2026-04-06)
Docs/source-pdfs/
Design/*
# ...except the two PM-inbox files the keep-work-flowing-cc task tracks (THR-650).
# (Uses `Design/*`, not `Design/`, so git can re-include files below an otherwise-ignored dir.)
!Design/briefing.md
!Design/user-actions.md
!Design/retros/
# ...and the approved design mockups (THR-980).
#
# `Design/mockups/2026-07-26-nudge-model-encounters.html` is the visual bar THR-969,
# THR-971 and THR-972 are all measured against, and THR-969 cites line ranges in it.
# It had sat on disk since 2026-07-26 while `Design/*` hid it from every git query,
# so THR-980's forensics ("not on main, not on ops, not in any branch's history")
# were blind by construction and reasonably concluded it had never existed. An
# acceptance bar that only one machine can see is not an acceptance bar.
#
# The assets need their own negation because `*.png` above ignores them a second
# time, and re-including the directory does not re-include files a separate
# pattern excludes.
!Design/mockups/
!Design/mockups/**/*.png
# ...and the quest-hook research corpus (THR-1217).
#
# `Design/research/quest-hooks/` is the 1,200-situation idea bank that step 0d of the
# nudge authoring spec tells every factory run to draw from. It was written 2026-07-31
# and `Design/*` hid all 13 files from every git query for the whole of its life —
# the identical failure the `mockups/` re-include above was added to fix, repeated in
# the folder next door five days later. `git status` read clean throughout, so no
# sweep ever flagged it; the spec's `or Hook: original` fallback always passed, so the
# gate reported success while the corpus it exists to consult was unreadable
# (impediment #720). Christian approved publication in chat 2026-08-24.
#
# Scoped to `research/` deliberately. `Design/*` must NOT be widened: `briefing.md`
# and `user-actions.md` are re-included one at a time above for the same reason, and
# the repo is public, so an over-broad negation here publishes by accident.
!Design/research/
!Design/research/**
# ...and the impediment dashboard's TEMPLATE — but deliberately NOT its render.
#
# `Design/impediment-dashboard.html` is generated from this template plus
# `Docs/impediments.md` on every `prebuild`. It was committed until THR-916, which
# made it a tracked file whose ~6,200 data lines changed on every impediment
# append. `Docs/impediments.md` tolerates that because it is `merge=union`; HTML
# has no such driver, so the moment any impediment-appending PR merged, every other
# open PR conflicted on this one file and the resolutions re-staled each other —
# four hand-resolutions across three PRs in one 40-minute run.
#
# Do NOT `git add -f` the rendered dashboard back (impediment #139 is how it got
# committed the first time). It is reproducible from committed inputs, and
# `check:generated-freshness` asserts both that the generator still produces it and
# that it stays untracked.
!Design/impediment-dashboard.template.html

# The assembled project-status page — generated, deliberately untracked (THR-1016).
#
# Same reasoning as the impediment dashboard above, reached from the other end. The
# page is assembled by `prebuild` from the one-file-per-entry fragments in
# `Docs/status/`, which ARE committed and are what a closeout writes. Committing the
# assembly puts every closeout PR back on one shared anchor: each one inserts at the
# top of `## Current Focus` and drops the tail to hold the line cap, so any two open
# closeout PRs conflicted by construction. Measured 2026-08-07 — PRs #1322, #1326,
# #1327 sat DIRTY for 17, 18 and 20 hours conflicting only in closeout docs, and
# #1322 had to be hand-resolved twice in one session.
#
# No merge driver fixes it: `union` duplicates the rewritten lines (why THR-691
# excluded this file), `ours` is not built-in and needs `.git/config` no repo file
# can ship, and GitHub's server-side merge ignores `.gitattributes` outright. Not
# sharing the write is the only thing that survives all three.
#
# Read the entries in `Docs/status/` (one file per ticket, newest-first by name), or
# run `npm run generate-project-status` for the assembled page. Do NOT `git add -f`
# it back — `check:generated-freshness` asserts both that the generator still
# produces it and that it stays untracked.
Docs/project-status.md

# Operational exhaust left behind in a working tree after publication (THR-1056).
#
# Every scheduled lane writes its dated report into its working tree and publishes it
# with `scripts/ops-publish.sh`, which commits to the `ops` branch via git plumbing and
# deliberately checks nothing out (THR-947). That is what makes it safe to call from any
# worktree — and it is also why the local file always survives the publish. Nothing ever
# deleted it, so the home tree accumulated 23 strays by 2026-08-09 and 38 by 08-14,
# roughly 3/day. The producing prompts now delete their own file after a successful
# publish; these rules are the backstop for whatever still slips through.
#
# The backstop is load-bearing, not cosmetic, and the reason is a git behaviour worth
# stating: a fast-forward refuses to clobber an untracked file and aborts the whole
# sync — THR-937's exact failure, measured at 11 consecutive skips and 88 commits
# behind — but an *ignored* file is expendable, so git overwrites it silently and the
# sync proceeds. Verified both directions in a scratch repo on 2026-08-15: the
# non-ignored stray produced "untracked working tree files would be overwritten by
# merge ... Aborting", while the ignored one was not named at all and was overwritten
# in place. Ignoring these paths therefore converts a permanent autosync deadlock into
# a no-op.
#
# These patterns cannot hide the pre-cutover archive: `.gitignore` has no effect on
# already-tracked files, and the 55 report-shaped files committed under `Docs/ops/`
# stay tracked and fully visible. Nor can they break publication — `ops-publish.sh`
# stages with `hash-object`/`update-index --cacheinfo`, which do not consult
# `.gitignore` the way `git add` would. Post-cutover reports belong only on `ops`, so
# a report-shaped file under `Docs/ops/` in a working tree is debris by definition.
Docs/ops/orchestrator-*.md
Docs/ops/backlog-grooming-*.md
Docs/ops/weekly-hygiene-*.md

# The retrospective skill's intermediate "deterministic draft; revise into narrative
# form" scratch file. The final `retro-<date>.md` is committed and is the artifact;
# the draft is disposable and was never deleted, leaving one per weekly run.
Design/retros/retro-*-draft.md

